Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Extends the Phase 02 memory layer: run_record.py already wrote a full
per-task record (ordered tool calls, completion check, final status)
to a JSON file for human/debug reading — that stays as-is. What was
missing was being able to ask anything about run history across a
project without opening files one at a time: "how many of the last 10
runs failed", "did we already work on auth.py".

memory.py:
- New `runs` table (run_id PK, project_id FK, task, model, timestamps,
  final_status, final_answer, steps_taken, stalled, error, and the
  full tool_calls list as a JSON column), indexed on (project_id,
  started_at).
- save_run(project_id, run_dict): INSERT OR REPLACE keyed on run_id,
  so a run saved once on error and again at natural completion updates
  the same row rather than duplicating.
- get_run(run_id): full run including tool_calls, as a plain dict.
- list_runs(project_id, limit=20): cheap most-recent-first summaries
  (RunSummary dataclass) without the tool_calls payload.
- search_runs(project_id, text): matches against task or final_answer
  text, scoped to the project.

forge.py:
- run_task() calls memory.save_run() alongside the existing JSON
  write, best-effort (never fails an otherwise-completed task).
- New `recall_runs` tool (query, limit) so the model itself can check
  whether something was already attempted, and whether it succeeded,
  before redoing work — distinct from the existing `recall` tool,
  which is freeform fact/assumption/decision/task_summary text, not
  the structured run log.
- System prompt documents the new tool with a JSON example.

test_memory.py (10 new tests, TestRunStorage): round-trip of
tool_calls through save/get, idempotency on run_id, ordering, limit,
project scoping, substring search over task and final_answer text, and
that the stalled flag survives as an actual bool.

test_forge.py (2 new end-to-end tests): a completed run_task() call is
proven to land in the queryable runs table automatically (not just the
JSON file), and the recall_runs tool is driven through the real
tool-call pipeline to find a prior run by content, with the assertion
checking the actual tool result delivered back into the conversation
rather than the polished final answer (which the existing, correct
"don't auto-verify unrecognized request types" heuristic leaves
generic for a query type outside its known categories).

72/72 tests pass (60 prior + 12 new).

